INT. TUNA AUCTION HALL - PREDAWN

A cavernous refrigerated hall of wet concrete, fluorescent tubes, and rows of giant frozen tuna laid on pallets like torpedoes. Men in rubber boots drag hooks. Breath smokes. Prices are shouted in clipped dialect.

A STRANGER, 30s, in a rented parka and expensive shoes, picks his way down an aisle between the fish. He keeps glancing at a paper in his hand. Behind a stack of crates, an old broker named TAKUMI SHINODA-REYES, 60s, watches him with a cigarette dangling from his lip.

TAKUMI
Looking for something, friend?

STRANGER
A man named Osei.

TAKUMI
There are forty Oseis here.

STRANGER
This one owes my employer.

TAKUMI
They all do.

Takumi lights the cigarette, then points the burning tip toward a corner. There, hunched over a pallet, a heavyset man in an orange apron prods a tuna with a gaff. OSEI QUAYSON-LEE, 40s, doesn't look up.

STRANGER
(to Osei)
Mr. Quayson-Lee.

OSEI
Who's asking?

STRANGER
My name is Anders.

OSEI
That's a name, not an answer.

ANDERS
I work for Mr. Pruitt.

At the name, Osei's gaff stops. Takumi drops his cigarette. A woman in a fish-market smock, MIREILLE BOUCHARD-WU, 40s, appears from behind the pallet with a clipboard and a calculator. She stops, looks at Anders, then at Osei.

MIREILLE
(low)
Osei. Who is this?

OSEI
A friend of Pruitt's.

ANDERS
I'd say colleague.

MIREILLE
Pruitt's dead.

ANDERS
He was. Until Thursday.

A cold silence. Another auctioneer starts a call in the distance, a sing-song of numbers. The fluorescent tubes buzz. Osei sets down the gaff. His hand drifts toward his apron pocket, and Anders sees it.

ANDERS (CONT'D)
Don't.

OSEI
It's a sandwich.

MIREILLE ^
It's a sandwich.

Osei draws out a foil-wrapped sandwich and holds it up like a flag. The tension breaks. Takumi laughs, a harsh cough of a laugh. Anders lowers the hand he had raised to his own coat.

ANDERS
Fine. Mr. Pruitt wants his money by Friday.

OSEI
Pruitt can have his money Friday, if the tuna sells.

ANDERS
The tuna will sell.

MIREILLE
Then Friday.

An engine roars behind them. A forklift lurches around a stack of crates with a skinny kid at the wheel, KENJI FLORES-ADE, 20s, headphones on, eyes on his phone. He sees the group and slams the brakes. The forklift slews, and a pallet of frozen tuna tilts off its forks toward Anders.

KENJI
Look out!

Anders dives. The pallet crashes down inches from his shoes, and a hundred-pound fish skids across the concrete and fetches up against Takumi's boot.

TAKUMI
(deadpan)
Auction's open.

Anders lies on the wet floor, staring at the ceiling. Osei offers him half the sandwich.
